      {
        "id": "4929adf1-5538-4c9a-8654-22c5ca8caf81",
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "That explains everything — and changes the answer on both fronts:\n\n**1. Why the PC can't see it:** It's an Xbox 360 (RROD = 360's signature failure). The 360's USB ports are **host ports only** — they're for plugging controllers/drives *into* the console, never the other way around. There is no USB data or video mode to a PC. Windows will never enumerate it over USB, no matter what. That's confirmed by the scan: zero USB arrivals in the last 30 minutes, nothing under Microsoft's vendor ID.\n\n**2. The red ring itself.** How many lights?\n- **3 rings (the classic RROD)** = GPU solder-joint failure. Basically a dead board without repair.\n- **2 rings** = overheating (easiest to fix)\n- **1 ring** = hardware failure with an on-screen E-error code\n- **4 rings** = just AV cable not detected — console is actually fine\n\nIf it's the 3-ring, you can pull the **secondary error code** (the real diagnosis): console off → hold the **Sync** button → tap **Eject** → the ring flashes a pattern of 1–4 lights, repeated 4 times = 4 digits. Tell me the code and I can tell you exactly what failed.\n\nRealistic options, in order of sanity:\n\n| Option | Effort | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Clean + reseat, check fan/paste | 30 min | Only helps 2-ring/overheat cases |\n| Get the secondary code | 2 min | Tells us if it's worth anything |\n| Local repair shop (reflow/reball) | $40–80 | Maybe; 360 boards are old, results vary |\n| Replacement 360 (used) | $40–100 | Often cheaper than the repair |\n| **Xenia emulator on this PC** | Free | Your Iris Xe iGPU will struggle with 360 emulation honestly — it's CPU-heavy, doable but not great |\n| Towel trick | — | Don't. It's a 20-minute oven-bake that temporarily reflows solder and dies again; real fire risk |\n\nWhat's the secondary error code, or how many rings are lit?",
        "timestamp": 1793192330456,
        "model": "assistant-llm"
      },
