      {
        "id": "66586593-2965-4f0c-a832-96bbaa1a0bc5",
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "",
        "timestamp": 1793309311863,
        "model": "assistant-llm",
        "toolCalls": [
          {
            "id": "call_115fa42c9681475ab9b0ec8a",
            "name": "Write",
            "arguments": {
              "file_path": "~\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-localuser\\memory\\project_xbox360-tinkering-2026-10-29.md",
              "content": "---\nname: project-xbox360-tinkering-2026-10-29\ndescription: Jordan's fat Xbox 360 — \"RROD\" was a loose AV plug (4 rings = no video detected); boots fine; ICS network lane to workstation armed; JTAG/RGH3 tinker path scoped\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nJordan's old **fat Xbox 360** (2026-10-29) wired to workstation. Status at handoff:\n\n- **\"Red ring of death\" = 4 rings = AV-cable-not-detected, NOT a dead console.** Reseating the video plug at the console end fixed it; boots clean. (Fat-360 AV plug is deep/stiff — feels seated when it isn't.)\n- **USB is a permanent dead end**: 360 USB ports are host-only (controllers/drives), never enumerate to a PC. Verified zero Kernel-PnP arrivals under Microsoft VID 045E.\n- **Ethernet lane**: 360 → workstation built-in \"Ethernet\" port, link Up @100 Mbps (fat 360 = 10/100; gigabit peer = wrong device). **ICS enabled**: Ethernet 2 (router) public → Ethernet private, workstation = `192.168.137.1`. 360 hasn't joined — stuck at controller splash.\n- **Controller gate**: stock dash splash demands a controller signal; USB keyboards navigate *inside* the dash but usually can't pass the splash. workstation's Xbox One/Series BT pad is protocol-incompatible — needs a real 360 controller (2×AA works, or wired ~$15).\n- **Tinker path** (Jordan wants to hack it, not just play): JTAG if kernel ≤7371 (no modchip, resistors only) vs RGH3 (Pi Pico ~$4–8 doubles as NAND flasher + glitch chip). **NEVER accept a dashboard update** — kills old-kernel JTAG eligibility. Check: System Settings → System Info → kernel version; blades vs NXE dash.\n\n**How to apply:** next touch = controller → network Automatic → catch on 192.168.137.x → kernel check → parts list (Pi Pico, 30 AWG, iron). Disable ICS on workstation when the lane isn't needed (it's live at handoff).\n"
            }
          }
        ]
      },
